# XSH sem_destroy()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Using a destroyed semaphore, destroying one with blocked threads, and
# destroying a named semaphore are undefined and not tried. The EINVAL
# and EBUSY errors are permissions ("may fail").

assertion  XSH.sem_destroy.001
anchor     functions/sem_destroy.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/sem_destroy.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       sem_destroy() of an initialized semaphore on which no thread is
           blocked (value 0, and value 3) returns 0.
test       t/001-destroy.c

assertion  XSH.sem_destroy.002
anchor     functions/sem_destroy.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/sem_init.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       After sem_destroy(), a new sem_init() on the same object works
           and gives a semaphore with the newly given value (it is not the
           old value), usable with sem_post(), and it can be destroyed
           again.
test       t/002-reinit.c

assertion  XSH.sem_destroy.003
anchor     functions/sem_destroy.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       sem_destroy() of a semaphore on which a thread waited, and was
           released by a post and has returned, returns 0.
test       t/003-after-waiter.c

assertion  XSH.sem_destroy.004
anchor     functions/sem_destroy.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       Where an unnamed semaphore uses a file descriptor, sem_destroy()
           closes it; observable as an unchanged lowest free descriptor
           after twenty init/destroy cycles (after one warming cycle of each kind, pshared 0 and non-zero).
note       A system that holds one descriptor for all semaphores keeps it
           open across the warming cycle too.
test       t/004-no-descriptor-left.c
